  "query_preview": "..\n\nMild and dry conditions define the start of the forecast period \ntoday with temperature readings currently in the mid 40s across SE \nMichigan. Light southwest flow this morning will veer to the west-\nnorthwest by mid day as a surface trough glances across the region. \nNot much of an impact to sensible weather aside from the wind shift, \nalthough very strong isentropic downglide will reinforce static \nstability aloft and dry weather. Boundary layer growth gradually \nerodes the inversion to bring high temperatures into the low 60s.\n\nA more active pattern emerges for the second half of the week, \nbeginning with a Pacific wave which was just sampled over the \nPacific Northwest. The pattern amplifies substantially Wednesday as \na ~100 knot jet streak digs south and sends the PV anomaly into the \nsouthern OH/TN Valleys by 00z Thursday. This drags the main height \nfall center south of the state line, but does position SE Michigan \nwithin a prolonged period of moist isentropic ascent Wednesday \nafternoon-early Thursday morning. With cold air and a second wave \ncrashing in from the north, there is potential for FGEN banding to \ndevelop within the broader rain shield. If this occurs, a narrow \nswath could see over a quarter inch of rain before lake effect kicks \nin. The added cloud cover and beginnings of cold advection hold highs\nWednesday in the low-mid 50s. \n\nThe low deepens and matures as it moves into the mid-Atlantic \nThursday morning, in part due to the trailing wave phasing into it. \nThe first implication of this is an increase in the northerly \ngradient wind, which will become breezy (gusts of 25-30 mph). Second \nis the development of an inverted trough axis that will be one of \nthe focused opportunities for lake enhanced showers. Thursday is \nwhere precipitation type uncertainty first comes into play. Forcing\nalong the surface trough may become very concentrated, producing\nstrong upward vertical motion that corresponds to intense\nprecipitation rates and diabatic cooling potential. On the other\nhand, there are still differences in the timing and magnitude of the\ntrough along with the role of a warmer marine layer. For now, will\nadvertise potential for melting snowflakes to mix in as early as \nThursday.\n\nOnce this trough sweeps through, a more aggressive surge of cold \nadvection follows with h8 temperatures quickly dropping into the \nnegative single digits and fully activating the lake effect response \nfor Friday. Wet bulb zero heights gradually lower through the day \nbelow 2.0 kft agl as post-low winds back to the northwest and advect \ndrier dewpoints into SE Michigan, increasing potential melting\nsnowflakes to reach the ground. Forecast soundings show impressive \nmoisture depths above 10.0 kft agl for Friday with steepening low \nlevel lapse rates to support a convective/lake enhanced shower \nenvironment. Certainly some diabatic cooling potential here as well, \nwhich allows for the snowflake potential despite highs reaching the \nlow 40s. A more localized detail is the Lake Huron convergence band \nthat appears in the bulk of model guidance. Marine layer influence \nwill again struggle to get cold enough for snow, although diabatic \ncooling can be sneaky in these early season events. Moisture depths \ngradually decrease through the day Saturday while winds shift more to\nthe northwest, which generally shuts down precipitation chances. The\ncooler airmass however sticks around through the weekend, with highs\nThursday through the weekend in the 40s.",
